Z||000000||baptistheart||05-21-2001||10:03 PM||||It all started when my 85 4 cyl. fiero did not start. I thought it was the ignition switch so i dropped the steering collom,when i put it back up i got a pink wire smashed inbetween the frame and collom. the result was a bunch of smoke coming from the engine compartment. now i have absolutly no electricity throughout the car can someone please help i love driving this car and i need to earn my wifes respect back. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||11
Z||000001||PBJ||05-21-2001||10:09 PM||||sounds like the fusable link at the battery junction box.  remove battery side vent pannel and tug on the wires bolted to the little juction box beside the battery.<P>------------------<BR>   <BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Pete's_GT.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||baptistheart||05-22-2001||12:20 AM||||Thanks i'll try it. Hope it works There are alot of wires in the car and i had no idea where to start. i was hoping it wasent my computer.||||reg||1
Z||000003||baptistheart||05-25-2001||10:53 AM||||I have my electricity back! but my car still won't turn over can anyone tell me which wirewires run to the starter from the ignition switch I've tried another switch and no go I've also shorted between the two posts on the solenoid and it does start.||||reg||1
Z||000004||PBJ||05-25-2001||11:51 AM||||if it does not turn over when you jump the posts at the starter, check for main feed at starter. or a bad starter.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Ken Wittlief||05-25-2001||01:50 PM||||he said it does turn over when he jumps the starter<P>   you either have a toasted wire in the steering column, or another fuzable link back onthe side of the engine compartment is out.<P>   I dont think the starter engagment circuit uses a fuze ,i think it uses a fuzible link, but check your fuze box just to make sure there are no blown fuzes in there too.||||reg||1
Z||000006||baptistheart||05-25-2001||05:06 PM||||I figured it was a wire on the column (no blown fuses ) but i need to know what color the wire it is so i can test it.does anyone know wire colors for the ignition switch?||||reg||1
Z||000007||Ken Wittlief||05-26-2001||01:01 AM||||I have the factory service manual from the library for the '86 model year - its a good bet the wiring for the ignition is the same on yours.<P>  It shows a red fusible link at the battery junction box supplys power to the ignition switch. If you have no power at the switch, nothing lights up on the dash, no radio, wipers, nothing, then that fusible links is probally blown.<P>  If you are not familiar with them, they look just like a wire with a fat - buldge part about an inch or so long in the wire.<P>  At the ignition switch the red wire is the one that comes from that fusible link.<P>  the yellow wire from the ignition switch is the start circuit. If you have a mannytranny it goes to the clutch start switch, then to the starter solenoid.<P>if you have an auto tranny the yellow wire goes to the gear selector cuttout switch - must be in park or neutral to start, and then to the solenoid.<P>   thats it! nothing in the fuse box should affect the ignition start path. If you pinched a wire in the steering column then you must have:<P>  blown the red fusible link<BR>  melted the red wire from the link to the ignition switch<BR>  melted the contact for the start position of the ignition switch<BR>  or melted the yellow wire that goes from there.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Ken Wittlief||05-26-2001||01:05 AM||||I just noticed you said you pinched the pink wire. That is the run wire and it goes to the ignition coil and control module. If that was grounded in the steering column the you must have blown the red fusible link, the red wire, or the ignition switch itself.||||reg||1
Z||000009||baptistheart||05-26-2001||07:49 AM||||Thanks to both of you that was all the info I wanted it should work i appreciate it.||||reg||14
